{% extends "../_guide.njk" %}

{% block guideContent %}
  <h3 class="govuk-heading-s">What we will do with what you tell us</h3>

  <p class="govuk-body">We will read what everyone has told us and look for things people said that are the same.</p>

  <p class="govuk-body">Then we will share this with the team.</p>

  <p class="govuk-body">Using what we have learned we will [what will your team do next].</p>

  <h3 class="govuk-heading-s">How to get support if you need it after taking part</h3>

  <p class="govuk-body">Sometimes thinking about our experiences can bring up difficult feelings. It may make you feel like you need to talk to someone or get support. There are many helplines available with trained people who are ready to listen. They will not judge you, they can help you make sense of what you are feeling.</p>

  <ul class="govuk-list govuk-list--bullet">
    <li><strong>Samaritans:</strong> To talk about anything that is upsetting to you, you can call 116 123 which is free from any phone. It is available 24 hours a day, 365 days a year.</li>
    <li><strong>SANEline:</strong> If you are experiencing poor mental health, or supporting someone else you can call 0300 304 7000. It is available from 4:30pm to 10:30pm every day.</li>
    <li><strong>National Suicide Prevention Helpline UK:</strong> A supportive listening service for anyone with thoughts of suicide. You can call 0800 689 5652 between 6pm and midnight every day.</li>
    <li><strong>Campaign Against Living Miserably (CALM):</strong> If you are struggling and need to talk you can call 0800 58 58 58 between 5pm and midnight every day or use their webchat service.</li>
    <li><strong>Shout:</strong> If you would prefer not to talk but want some mental health support, you can text SHOUT to 85258. They offer a confidential text service, 24 hours a day, 7 days a week, if you are in crisis and need immediate help.</li>
  </ul>
{% endblock %}
